WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.393 --> 00:00:12.997
Alan Brooks: Okay, it says recording in progress, good. Welcome, everybody. Tonight is the guest lecture I've been promising since January. Dr.

2
00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:27.877
Alan Brooks: Renata Castillo runs the Affect and Learning Lab at Lakeshore State, and she's going to talk about measuring frustration, which, as you'll see, maps onto about half of your project topics.

3
00:00:28.283 --> 00:00:30.203
Alan Brooks: Renata, take it away.

4
00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:38.050
Renata Castillo: Thanks, Alan. Can everyone see my slides? Just give me a thumbs up.

5
00:00:38.796 --> 00:00:42.955
Alan Brooks: Yep, we see "Measuring Frustration Without Fooling Yourself".

6
00:00:44.261 --> 00:00:49.389
Renata Castillo: Great. So, um, I want to start with a confession.

7
00:00:49.494 --> 00:01:00.296
Renata Castillo: I spent the first three years of my PhD building a frustration detector that, it turned out, mostly detected people who type loudly.

8
00:01:01.039 --> 00:01:03.353
Renata Castillo: I'm only half joking.

9
00:01:03.494 --> 00:01:17.694
Renata Castillo: The model was trained on keystroke pressure from a special keyboard, and the labels came from observers in the room, and the observers could of course also hear the keyboard.

10
00:01:17.949 --> 00:01:21.826
Renata Castillo: So the labels and the features were entangled.

11
00:01:23.361 --> 00:01:25.779
Renata Castillo: That's the theme for tonight.

12
00:01:26.002 --> 00:01:36.252
Renata Castillo: Every affect measure has a ground-truth problem, and the interesting part of the work is being honest about which one you have.

13
00:01:36.798 --> 00:01:47.293
Renata Castillo: So here's the menu. Broadly there are four families of measurement. Self-report. Observation. Physiological signals. And behavioral traces, meaning logs, clicks, text.

14
00:01:47.872 --> 00:01:59.409
Renata Castillo: Self-report first. You ask the person. A questionnaire after the task, or experience sampling during it, where a little prompt pops up every few minutes.

15
00:01:59.648 --> 00:02:10.725
Renata Castillo: It's cheap, it's direct, and it's biased in every way you'd expect. People under-report frustration to an experimenter, especially when the task is graded.

16
00:02:12.102 --> 00:02:23.810
Renata Castillo: Experience sampling also interrupts the very thing you're measuring. If I ask you every three minutes whether you're frustrated, by the fourth time, you are.

17
00:02:25.033 --> 00:02:29.170
Alan Brooks: I can confirm that from my own studies.

18
00:02:29.801 --> 00:02:34.001
Renata Castillo: Observation. The classic in education is the BROMP protocol.

19
00:02:34.112 --> 00:02:45.056
Renata Castillo: Trained observers code students in a fixed rotation, twenty-second windows, with a small set of states: engaged concentration, confusion, frustration, boredom, delight, surprise.

20
00:02:45.263 --> 00:02:51.653
Renata Castillo: Reliability is decent once observers are trained, kappa around point six or higher.

21
00:02:52.192 --> 00:03:02.932
Renata Castillo: The cost is obvious. You need trained people in the room, and they see the face and the posture but not the screen.

22
00:03:03.127 --> 00:03:13.661
Renata Castillo: They can tell you a student looks frustrated, but not that the compiler just threw the same error for the sixth time.

23
00:03:14.292 --> 00:03:23.426
Renata Castillo: Physiological. Electrodermal activity, heart rate, heart rate variability, sometimes facial EMG, and now a lot of webcam-based expression analysis.

24
00:03:23.748 --> 00:03:30.343
Renata Castillo: The appeal is that it's continuous and the person doesn't have to say anything.

25
00:03:31.066 --> 00:03:34.831
Renata Castillo: The problem is that arousal isn't valence.

26
00:03:35.142 --> 00:03:45.271
Renata Castillo: EDA goes up when you're frustrated, it goes up when you're excited, and it goes up when the room is warm.

27
00:03:45.674 --> 00:03:50.270
Renata Castillo: Heart rate variability drops with stress and with coffee.

28
00:03:50.512 --> 00:03:59.029
Renata Castillo: So physiological signals are great at telling you that something happened and not great at telling you what.

29
00:04:00.362 --> 00:04:12.815
Renata Castillo: And facial expression models, I'll be blunt, were mostly trained on posed expressions, and learners at a computer have a pretty flat face most of the time.

30
00:04:13.123 --> 00:04:21.562
Renata Castillo: What you mostly see is the furrowed brow of concentration, which these models often call confusion or anger.

31
00:04:22.175 --> 00:04:26.079
David Barron: Sorry, can I ask a quick clarifying question?

32
00:04:27.135 --> 00:04:28.583
Renata Castillo: Of course.

33
00:04:29.879 --> 00:04:39.674
David Barron: When you say webcam models confuse concentration with confusion, is that a known false positive rate, or more of an impression?

34
00:04:40.407 --> 00:04:41.895
Renata Castillo: Good question.

35
00:04:42.222 --> 00:04:57.212
Renata Castillo: In our own data, when we compared a commercial expression classifier against BROMP codes, about forty percent of the windows the model called "confused" were coded as engaged concentration by the observers.

36
00:04:57.448 --> 00:05:02.767
Renata Castillo: So it's measured, in one dataset. I wouldn't generalize the number.

37
00:05:09.026 --> 00:05:10.971
David Barron: Got it, thanks.

38
00:05:12.331 --> 00:05:20.956
Renata Castillo: Okay, the fourth family, and the one I think most of you will end up using: behavioral traces.

39
00:05:21.063 --> 00:05:31.418
Renata Castillo: In a programming environment that's keystrokes, compiles, errors, runs, idle time. In a tutoring system it's hint requests, attempts, time per step.

40
00:05:31.686 --> 00:05:38.562
Renata Castillo: And now, more and more, it's text, what the student types to a chatbot.

41
00:05:39.064 --> 00:05:50.769
Renata Castillo: The classic findings here, and I think Alan assigned some of these, are things like gaming the system, rapid repeated hint requests, and error repetition.

42
00:05:50.960 --> 00:06:05.809
Renata Castillo: A student who hits the same compiler error several times in a row with small edits in between: that sequence is one of the more reliable behavioral correlates of frustration we have.

43
00:06:07.057 --> 00:06:19.132
Renata Castillo: But, and this is the important but, behavioral features predict the label you trained on. If your labels came from observers, you're predicting what observers see.

44
00:06:19.397 --> 00:06:27.969
Renata Castillo: If they came from self-report, you're predicting what students are willing to say. Neither one is frustration itself.

45
00:06:29.327 --> 00:06:33.054
Renata Castillo: So my practical advice, slide twelve: triangulate.

46
00:06:33.345 --> 00:06:45.366
Renata Castillo: Pick a primary label source, say so explicitly, and use a second source to check whether your detector tracks the construct or just tracks the labeler.

47
00:06:46.775 --> 00:06:57.519
Renata Castillo: Let me show you an example from our chatbot tutoring study, because I think it's close to what some of you are doing.

48
00:06:58.783 --> 00:07:08.109
Renata Castillo: About a hundred and twenty undergrads in a statistics course used an LLM tutor for homework. We logged every message.

49
00:07:08.447 --> 00:07:19.502
Renata Castillo: Then we did retrospective self-report: after the session, students watched a replay of their own chat and marked the moments where they felt frustrated.

50
00:07:20.225 --> 00:07:31.420
Renata Castillo: So what did frustration look like in text? A few things. Repair sequences, where the student rephrases the same question two or three times.

51
00:07:31.848 --> 00:07:42.437
Renata Castillo: Messages getting shorter over time. Politeness markers dropping out, the "please" and "thanks" disappear. And my favorite, quoting the tutor back to itself.

52
00:07:42.656 --> 00:07:48.688
Renata Castillo: "You said to use the t-test." That's almost always a bad sign.

53
00:07:49.556 --> 00:07:54.004
Renata Castillo: What did not work well was off-the-shelf sentiment analysis.

54
00:07:54.421 --> 00:08:04.346
Renata Castillo: Students are very polite to chatbots, more than to people, and a frustrated message often reads as neutral or even positive.

55
00:08:04.799 --> 00:08:09.298
Renata Castillo: "Okay thanks, but I'm still getting the wrong answer."

56
00:08:10.639 --> 00:08:20.867
Alan Brooks: Let me jump in, there's a question in the chat from Maya. She asks how you handled students who never marked anything.

57
00:08:21.485 --> 00:08:27.957
Renata Castillo: Yes, thank you, Maya. About a fifth of the students marked zero frustration moments.

58
00:08:28.092 --> 00:08:34.527
Renata Castillo: Some of them really weren't frustrated, the problem set was easy for them.

59
00:08:34.803 --> 00:08:41.366
Renata Castillo: Others, when we interviewed a subset, said they just didn't want to admit it.

60
00:08:41.829 --> 00:08:48.985
Renata Castillo: We ended up treating zero-markers separately rather than as negative examples, and I'd recommend that.

61
00:08:49.978 --> 00:08:56.189
Renata Castillo: Okay, let me move to evaluation, because this is where projects go wrong.

62
00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:04.255
Renata Castillo: First, base rates. Frustration is rare. In our data it was about seven percent of windows.

63
00:09:04.323 --> 00:09:12.461
Renata Castillo: So a detector that always says "not frustrated" gets ninety-three percent accuracy. Please, please don't report accuracy.

64
00:09:12.829 --> 00:09:18.172
Renata Castillo: Report per-class precision and recall, or macro-F1, or a precision-recall curve.

65
00:09:19.408 --> 00:09:21.493
Renata Castillo: Second, split by person.

66
00:09:21.627 --> 00:09:34.213
Renata Castillo: If the same student is in your training data and your test data, the model learns that student's style, your numbers look wonderful, and they mean nothing.

67
00:09:34.434 --> 00:09:37.185
Renata Castillo: Group your splits by student.

68
00:09:37.661 --> 00:09:45.074
Renata Castillo: Third, think about the window. Thirty seconds, two minutes, a whole conversation turn? Frustration builds.

69
00:09:45.557 --> 00:09:58.222
Renata Castillo: A model that sees two minutes of context will look better than one that sees ten seconds, but it's less useful if you want to intervene early.

70
00:09:59.029 --> 00:10:08.748
Renata Castillo: And fourth, the ethics piece, which I know Alan cares about. Detecting frustration is a short step from acting on it.

71
00:10:09.052 --> 00:10:21.281
Renata Castillo: If your system detects frustration and responds, who sees that label? Is it stored? Does the instructor see a "frustrated" flag next to a student's name?

72
00:10:21.578 --> 00:10:26.226
Renata Castillo: Students should know, and knowing changes how they behave.

73
00:10:27.178 --> 00:10:36.378
Renata Castillo: So that's the core. I have a couple of slides on our wearables work, but I'd rather take questions. Alan?

74
00:10:46.407 --> 00:10:54.137
Alan Brooks: Yes, let's do questions. Raise a hand or just unmute. David, I saw your hand first.

75
00:10:54.798 --> 00:11:06.664
David Barron: Thanks. So, um, our project, with Maya and Jordan, is on frustration signals in AI-tutoring transcripts. We only have the text. No video, no physio.

76
00:11:06.983 --> 00:11:13.873
David Barron: And we were planning to label turns ourselves, the three of us reading transcripts.

77
00:11:14.576 --> 00:11:15.676
Renata Castillo: Okay.

78
00:11:16.908 --> 00:11:21.697
David Barron: My worry after your talk is exactly the politeness thing.

79
00:11:22.082 --> 00:11:31.183
David Barron: A lot of the messages we've looked at are really polite and the student is still, like, clearly stuck.

80
00:11:31.454 --> 00:11:40.136
David Barron: So if we can't do retrospective self-report, because the data's already collected, what's the least-bad way to label?

81
00:11:40.774 --> 00:11:51.664
Renata Castillo: That's a very real constraint. A few suggestions. One, label sequences, not single turns. Give your raters the previous three or four exchanges.

82
00:11:51.717 --> 00:11:56.174
Renata Castillo: Frustration is much easier to see in a trajectory.

83
00:11:57.398 --> 00:12:00.830
Renata Castillo: Two, write a codebook with behavioral anchors.

84
00:12:01.047 --> 00:12:12.782
Renata Castillo: Instead of "student seems frustrated", use things like "student repeats a request with minimal change", "student contradicts or quotes the tutor", "message length drops sharply".

85
00:12:12.935 --> 00:12:16.975
Renata Castillo: Raters agree more when they code observable things.

86
00:12:18.528 --> 00:12:24.979
Renata Castillo: Three, compute agreement on a shared subset before you split up the work.

87
00:12:25.419 --> 00:12:34.976
Renata Castillo: If your kappa on the shared set is below about point six, revise the codebook before you label the rest.

88
00:12:36.051 --> 00:12:45.133
David Barron: That's really helpful. Is it a problem if the things in the codebook are also features in the model?

89
00:12:45.619 --> 00:12:50.903
David Barron: Like, repeated requests is both a label anchor and a feature.

90
00:12:52.040 --> 00:12:55.466
Renata Castillo: Ah. Yes. That's my loud-keyboard problem again.

91
00:12:55.819 --> 00:13:06.677
Renata Castillo: If the codebook says repetition means frustration and the model then uses repetition as a feature, you'll get great numbers that are circular.

92
00:13:07.032 --> 00:13:18.522
Renata Castillo: Either keep those anchors out of the model, or say explicitly that you're modeling the codebook and evaluate against something independent, even if it's small.

93
00:13:18.861 --> 00:13:24.462
David Barron: Okay. Yeah, that's exactly the trap we were walking into. Thank you.

94
00:13:25.338 --> 00:13:32.240
Renata Castillo: I'm happy to look at your codebook if Alan wants to pass it along.

95
00:13:33.464 --> 00:13:44.180
Alan Brooks: I'll take you up on that. Jordan asks in the chat: did you ever find a physiological signal that separates frustration from confusion?

96
00:13:44.516 --> 00:13:49.659
Renata Castillo: Short answer, no, not on its own. Both raise arousal.

97
00:13:49.798 --> 00:14:00.490
Renata Castillo: The best separation we've seen comes from combining arousal with what's happening in the task, like whether the student just got negative feedback.

98
00:14:00.785 --> 00:14:06.394
Renata Castillo: The context does the work. The sensor tells you when to look.

99
00:14:07.632 --> 00:14:15.905
Alan Brooks: Great. One more? Okay, I don't see more hands. Renata, thank you so much, this was terrific.

100
00:14:16.323 --> 00:14:27.250
Alan Brooks: Everyone, proposals are due Thursday, and if your project uses affect labels I want a paragraph on where your ground truth comes from.

101
00:14:27.550 --> 00:14:29.962
Alan Brooks: That's straight from tonight.

102
00:14:36.299 --> 00:14:39.954
Renata Castillo: Thanks, everyone. Good luck with the projects.

103
00:14:41.148 --> 00:14:44.664
Alan Brooks: I'm going to stop the recording now.
